VALUES (
  '2026/10/22/gas-pump-is-setting-inflation-expectations-while-basket-cools',
  'behaviour',
  'article',
  'The Gas Pump Is Setting Inflation Expectations While the Basket Cools',
  'Gasoline is up 27.8% from a year ago and the Bank of Canada core measures sit below 2%, yet consumer five-year inflation expectations just jumped to 3.91%. Behavioural research explains why the pump wins.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>Gasoline rose 27.8% year over year in September,</strong><span> while consumer prices excluding gasoline rose 2.2% and the Bank of Canada core measures eased to 1.8% (CPI-trim) and 1.9% (CPI-median).</span></li>
<li><strong>Consumer five-year inflation expectations jumped to 3.91%,</strong><span> from 3.39% in the second quarter, and now match elevated short-term expectations in the latest Bank of Canada survey.</span></li>
<li><strong>The availability heuristic explains the gap:</strong><span> Tversky and Kahneman showed in 1973 that people judge frequency by how easily examples come to mind, and a posted gasoline price is the easiest example in the economy.</span></li>
<li><strong>City inflation ran from 2.1% in Toronto to 6.1% in Whitehorse,</strong><span> so the inflation a household experiences depends heavily on where it shops and how it heats.</span></li>
<li><strong>Overnight swaps priced an 87% chance of an October 28 hike on Monday,</strong><span> up from about 30% a week earlier, after Governor Tiff Macklem said he expects the Bank to raise its inflation forecast.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>Statistics Canada reported on October 19 that gasoline was 27.8% more expensive in September than a year earlier. Excluding gasoline, consumer prices rose 2.2%. The two core measures the Bank of Canada watches most closely eased, with CPI-trim at 1.8% and CPI-median at 1.9%.</p>
<p>Households were answering a different question. In the Bank of Canada survey of consumer expectations, published the same day, five-year inflation expectations jumped more than half a point to 3.91% from 3.39% in the second quarter, rising to match elevated short-term expectations. Most interviews were completed before trade talks with the United States broke down, so the tariff fight cannot explain most of the jump.</p>
<p>A gap of just over two percentage points between the 1.9% core reading and the 3.91% households expect over five years is not evidence of irrationality. It is the predictable output of how people build an inflation estimate, and behavioural finance has been documenting that process for more than fifty years.</p>
<h2>The Availability Heuristic Is Doing the Arithmetic</h2>
<p>In 1973, Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman showed that people judge how common an event is by how easily examples come to mind, a shortcut they called the availability heuristic. It works when memory mirrors reality. It misleads when one kind of evidence is far more vivid than the rest.</p>
<p>Gasoline is the most vivid price in the economy. It is bought every week or two, it is posted in large digits on street corners, and it is paid at the moment of purchase. Gasoline rose 27.8%, and a driver is reminded of it each time the tank empties.</p>
<p>The basket tells a quieter story. Shelter costs rose 1.8% and food bought in stores rose 1.7%, the smallest annual gain since May 2024. Those prices arrive as a mortgage payment or a grocery receipt, with no number on a pole to announce the trend.</p>
<p>The grocery aisle carries its own distortion. In a 2021 paper in the Journal of Political Economy, Francesco D’Acunto, Ulrike Malmendier, Juan Ospina and Michael Weber found that the weight a consumer gives a price change depends on how often the good is bought, not on its share of spending, and that price increases loom larger than decreases. Groceries are still 33.0% more expensive than in September 2020, so a shopper can watch the rate of increase slow and still feel the level.</p>
<h2>Experienced Inflation Is Local, and So Is the Memory</h2>
<p>Ulrike Malmendier and Stefan Nagel reported in the Quarterly Journal of Economics in 2016 that individuals overweight the inflation they have personally lived through. Using 57 years of the Reuters and University of Michigan consumer survey, they found that differences in lived experience strongly predict differences in expected inflation. What a household remembers shapes what it expects.</p>
<p>Twelve of the 19 cities Statistics Canada reports ran above the national 3.1% in September, with readings from 2.1% in Toronto to 6.1% in Whitehorse that span twice the width of the Bank of Canada one-to-three-per-cent control range.</p>
<div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
<div style="background:#f5f5f5;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d0d0;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
<span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#111;letter-spacing:0.02em;">CANADA CPI BY CITY</span>
<span style="font-size:20px;font-weight:700;color:#111;">3.1%</span>
<span style="font-size:13px;color:#2e7d32;">▲ 0.1 PTS VS AUGUST</span>
<span style="font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-left:auto;">MONTHLY &nbsp;|&nbsp; 19 CITIES, SEPTEMBER 2026</span>
</div>
<div style="padding:12px 14px 8px;">
<script>
(function(){
  var _cs = document.currentScript;
  var NS = "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg";
  var FONT = "-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif";
  function el(tag, attrs, txt){
    var e = document.createElementNS(NS, tag);
    for (var k in attrs) e.setAttribute(k, attrs[k]);
    if (txt !== undefined) e.textContent = txt;
    return e;
  }
  function tx(x, y, s, anchor, size, fill, weight){
    return el("text", {x: x, y: y, "text-anchor": anchor, "font-size": size, "font-weight": weight || 400, fill: fill, "font-family": FONT}, s);
  }
  var svg = document.createElementNS(NS, "svg");
  svg.setAttribute("viewBox","0 0 680 300");
  svg.setAttribute("width","100%");
  var data = [{c:"Whitehorse",v:6.1},{c:"Charlottetown",v:5.6},{c:"Halifax",v:5.4},{c:"Saint John",v:4.8},{c:"Yellowknife",v:4.7},{c:"St. John’s",v:4.6},{c:"Winnipeg",v:4.2},{c:"Edmonton",v:3.5},{c:"Regina",v:3.4},{c:"Calgary",v:3.4},{c:"Victoria",v:3.4},{c:"Quebec City",v:3.3},{c:"Saskatoon",v:3.1},{c:"Montreal",v:3.0},{c:"Ottawa",v:3.0},{c:"Vancouver",v:3.0},{c:"Iqaluit",v:3.0},{c:"Thunder Bay",v:2.8},{c:"Toronto",v:2.1}];
  var NAT = 3.1, TARGET = 2.0;
  var margin = {left: 110, right: 24, top: 18, bottom: 46};
  var W = 680, H = 300;
  var PW = W - margin.left - margin.right;
  var PH = H - margin.top - margin.bottom;
  var n = data.length;
  var pitch = PH / n;
  var vals = data.map(function(d){ return d.v; });
  var lo = Math.floor((Math.min.apply(null, vals) - 0.4) * 2) / 2;
  var hi = Math.ceil((Math.max.apply(null, vals) + 0.4) * 2) / 2;
  function xp(v){ return margin.left + (v - lo) / (hi - lo) * PW; }
  function yp(i){ return margin.top + (i + 0.5) * pitch; }
  var plotL = margin.left, plotR = margin.left + PW, plotT = margin.top, plotB = margin.top + PH;

  // 1. gridlines: vertical ticks and faint row guides
  for (var t = Math.ceil(lo); t <= Math.floor(hi); t++){
    svg.appendChild(el("line", {x1: xp(t), x2: xp(t), y1: plotT, y2: plotB, stroke: "#ececec", "stroke-width": 0.5}));
  }
  for (var i = 0; i < n; i++){
    svg.appendChild(el("line", {x1: plotL, x2: plotR - 40, y1: yp(i), y2: yp(i), stroke: "#ececec", "stroke-width": 0.5, "stroke-dasharray": "1,3"}));
  }

  // 2. reference lines
  svg.appendChild(el("line", {x1: xp(TARGET), x2: xp(TARGET), y1: plotT - 2, y2: plotB, stroke: "#7a3030", "stroke-width": 1, "stroke-dasharray": "3,3"}));
  svg.appendChild(el("line", {x1: xp(NAT), x2: xp(NAT), y1: plotT - 2, y2: plotB, stroke: "#2e7d32", "stroke-width": 1, "stroke-dasharray": "3,3"}));

  // 4. axis lines (before dots so the dots paint on top)
  svg.appendChild(el("line", {x1: plotL, x2: plotR, y1: plotB, y2: plotB, stroke: "#d8d8d8", "stroke-width": 1}));
  svg.appendChild(el("line", {x1: plotL, x2: plotL, y1: plotT, y2: plotB, stroke: "#d8d8d8", "stroke-width": 1}));

  // 5. data dots
  for (var j = 0; j < n; j++){
    svg.appendChild(el("circle", {cx: xp(data[j].v), cy: yp(j), r: 3.5, fill: data[j].v > NAT ? "#8a3030" : "#4a5568"}));
  }

  // 6. gold pill on the highest city, left of the endpoint, dot stays at the data point
  var pillText = data[0].v.toFixed(1) + "%";
  var pillW = Math.ceil(pillText.length * 9 * 0.58) + 10;
  var pillH = 16;
  var lastX = xp(data[0].v), lastY = yp(0);
  var pillX = lastX - pillW - 6;
  var pillY = lastY - pillH / 2;
  if (pillX < plotL) pillX = plotL;
  svg.appendChild(el("rect", {x: pillX, y: pillY, width: pillW, height: pillH, rx: 3, fill: "#e8a825"}));
  svg.appendChild(tx(pillX + pillW / 2, pillY + pillH / 2 + 3, pillText, "middle", 9, "#111111", 700));

  // 7. labels and annotations
  for (var r = 0; r < n; r++){
    svg.appendChild(tx(margin.left - 5, yp(r) + 3, data[r].c, "end", 8.5, "#aaaaaa", 400));
    if (r !== 0) svg.appendChild(tx(plotR - 4, yp(r) + 3, data[r].v.toFixed(1) + "%", "end", 8.5, "#444444", 400));
  }
  for (var k = Math.ceil(lo); k <= Math.floor(hi); k++){
    svg.appendChild(tx(xp(k), plotB + 14, k.toFixed(1) + "%", "middle", 8, "#999999", 400));
  }
  svg.appendChild(tx(margin.left + PW / 2, plotB + 30, "Year-over-year change in consumer prices, September 2026", "middle", 8, "#999999", 400));
  svg.appendChild(tx(xp(TARGET) + 4, plotT - 6, "BOC 2% TARGET", "start", 7, "#7a3030", 700));
  svg.appendChild(tx(xp(NAT) + 4, plotT - 6, "NATIONAL 3.1%", "start", 7, "#2e7d32", 700));

  // annotation block, right-anchored, clear of every dot (all rows below 4.0 sit left of mid-plot)
  var note = ["4.0 point spread from", "Toronto to Whitehorse,", "twice the width of the", "BoC 1% to 3% range"];
  var noteX = plotR - 40;
  var noteY = yp(11);
  var noteW = 86;
  svg.appendChild(el("rect", {x: noteX - noteW - 4, y: noteY - 9, width: noteW + 8, height: note.length * 10 + 2, fill: "#ffffff"}));
  for (var q = 0; q < note.length; q++){
    svg.appendChild(tx(noteX, noteY + q * 10, note[q], "end", 8, "#444444", 400));
  }

  // legend, derived from one base x and flush with the plot boundary
  var legend = [{t: "AT OR BELOW", c: "#4a5568"}, {t: "ABOVE NATIONAL", c: "#8a3030"}];
  var lgx = plotR - 4;
  var lgy = plotT - 9;
  legend.forEach(function(it){
    var w = Math.ceil(it.t.length * 7.5 * 0.68);
    svg.appendChild(tx(lgx, lgy + 3, it.t, "end", 7.5, "#444444", 700));
    var cxl = lgx - w - 8;
    svg.appendChild(el("circle", {cx: cxl, cy: lgy, r: 3.5, fill: it.c}));
    lgx = cxl - 16;
  });

  _cs.parentNode.appendChild(svg);
})();
</script>
</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Statistics Canada, Consumer Price Index, September 2026, released October 19, 2026, via The Canadian Press. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Twelve of the 19 cities reported ran above the national rate, and Statistics Canada said the fuel oil increase weighed more heavily in Atlantic Canada, where that fuel is more widely used. Statistics Canada cautioned that city figures rest on small samples and can fluctuate widely.</p>
<p>Fuel oil explains part of the pattern. Excluding energy, inflation was highest in Nova Scotia at 3.3%, against a national 2.1%, and Statistics Canada said the fuel oil increase had a greater impact in Atlantic Canada. Halifax at 5.4% and Charlottetown and Summerside at 5.6% are living through a different inflation than Toronto at 2.1%.</p>
<p>City figures rest on small samples, so any single reading deserves caution. The direction is still the point: a household in Halifax and a household in Toronto are not reading the same inflation, yet both are asked about the same national target.</p>
<h2>Why the Expectation Matters More Than the Print</h2>
<p>A single inflation print is history. An expectation is a forecast that people act on, feeding into wage demands and pricing decisions, which is why the Bank of Canada tracks it closely. Roughly half of the firms in its Business Outlook Survey plan to keep passing some or all cost increases on to customers.</p>
<p>Markets moved on this logic within days. On October 19, overnight swaps priced an 87% chance of a quarter-point Bank of Canada hike on October 28, according to Bloomberg data cited by the Financial Post, up from about 30% a week earlier. Governor Tiff Macklem had told reporters on October 17 that he expects the Bank to raise its inflation forecast, with oil far above the level assumed in July.</p>
<p>The labour market complicates the picture. Canada shed 68,000 jobs in September, the second straight monthly decline, and unemployment rose to 6.5%. Households facing higher fuel costs and a less secure job market are not wrong to feel squeezed. The behavioural question is which signal they weight most when deciding what to do with their savings.</p>
<h2>Salience Fades Slowly, and October 28 Is the Next Test</h2>
<p>If lived experience is overweighted, as Malmendier and Nagel found, expectations formed during a year of 27.8% gasoline inflation are unlikely to reset the moment the pump price eases. The gap closes one of two ways: household expectations drift down toward the 1.8% to 1.9% core readings, or core inflation drifts up toward what households already expect.</p>
<p>The Bank of Canada announces its rate decision on October 28 alongside a full Monetary Policy Report. Its updated inflation forecast will show how the central bank reads the gap, with the policy rate held at 2.25% since October 2025.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients are reading the pump and the grocery receipt as proof that inflation has not been beaten, and talk of a Bank of Canada rate hike has added a second worry underneath: that borrowing costs are about to rise again. The feeling is a loss of control over everyday costs, not a view about the CPI basket. Clients renewing a mortgage within a year feel it most sharply. Clients in Atlantic Canada have a point, since their local inflation is running well above the national rate.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">I understand why the price at the pump feels like proof that inflation is getting away from everyone. It is a fair reaction, because gasoline really is up 27.8% from a year ago and you see that number every week. Here is what I want you to hold next to it. Without gasoline, prices rose 2.2%, and the two measures the Bank of Canada trusts most for underlying inflation both came in under 2%. Both things are true at once. The Bank makes its next rate decision on October 28, and a hike is possible, so I would rather we look at where a rate move would actually touch your plan than react to a headline. Your plan was built to work through years when inflation runs above and below target, and nothing in the latest data changes your long-term numbers. Let us look at the two places inflation could really reach you, your mortgage renewal and your withdrawals, and decide together whether anything needs to change.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Retirees and near-retirees drawing income, who tend to anchor on pump and grocery prices and may want to raise cash or abandon long-term assumptions, and clients with a mortgage renewal in the next 12 months who are exposed to a possible October 28 hike.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients in Atlantic Canada and the territories, where local readings of 5.4% in Halifax, 5.6% in Charlottetown and Summerside, and 6.1% in Whitehorse justify more of the worry than the national figure does. They need acknowledgement before data.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with Canadian energy holdings, and savers in short-term deposits whose rates would reprice upward if the Bank hikes.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull every client with a mortgage renewal in the next 12 months and note the renewal date beside the October 28 decision.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag clients in Atlantic Canada and the territories and open each call with their local inflation figure before the national one.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check the inflation assumption in each retiree plan against the 1.8% and 1.9% core readings and the 3.91% consumer expectation, and record which one the plan uses.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Log any client who has asked to change allocation because of fuel or grocery prices, and document the conversation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a one-line summary of headline (3.1%), ex-gasoline (2.2%) and core (1.8% to 1.9%) inflation to send after each call.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Calendar October 28 for the Bank of Canada decision and Monetary Policy Report, and book follow-up calls for that afternoon.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Inflation, the pump and your plan<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Thank you for talking through the inflation numbers with me today. As promised, here is the short version.<br><br>
Gasoline is up 27.8% from a year ago, which is why prices feel so high. Excluding gasoline, prices rose 2.2% in September, and the Bank of Canada measures of underlying inflation came in at 1.8% and 1.9%. Both are real, and they point in different directions.<br><br>
The Bank of Canada announces its next rate decision on October 28. A rate increase is possible, so I will review any mortgage renewal or borrowing plans that could be affected, and I will check in with you that afternoon.<br><br>
Your long-term plan was built to hold up through years when inflation runs both above and below target. If anything in your situation has changed, please tell me and we will adjust deliberately rather than react to a headline.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors who have moved to cash, GICs or gold since the gasoline spike:</strong> they are reacting to the most visible price with no framework for the gap between 27.8% at the pump and 1.9% core inflation.</p>
<p><strong>Homeowners with variable-rate debt or a renewal within 12 months:</strong> overnight swaps priced an 87% chance of a Bank of Canada hike on October 19, and few are modelling the payment.</p>
<p><strong>Residents of Atlantic Canada and the territories:</strong> local inflation runs from 4.6% in St. John’s to 6.1% in Whitehorse, so the worry is justified and the need for a plan is real.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Inflation numbers came out Monday and gasoline is up nearly 28% from a year ago, but the core measures the Bank of Canada watches actually eased. I am calling people who manage their own investments to ask how they are reading that gap, and whether it has changed anything in their portfolio.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>Self-directed investors are building inflation forecasts from the most visible price in the economy, which is exactly how Tversky and Kahneman showed in 1973 that people judge frequency when examples are vivid. Nobody is positioned to separate the pump from the basket for them, and the risk shows up as decisions: selling into cash at the wrong moment, or holding inflation hedges sized to a fear rather than a forecast.</p>
<p>An advisor offers a framework that exists before the headline: which inflation matters for this household, over what horizon, and at what cost if the assumption is wrong. A possible rate hike on October 28, with a Monetary Policy Report the same day, gives a natural reason to ask for a conversation.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<div class="checklist-item">How are you reading the inflation numbers that came out Monday?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">When you think about prices over the next five years, what number do you have in mind, and what is it based on?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Has anything about the way your money is invested changed since gas prices started rising?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Who do you talk to when a headline makes you want to change your portfolio?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Do you have a mortgage, line of credit or renewal coming up that a rate increase on October 28 would affect?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">If your investments were 10% lower next quarter, what would you do, and has that plan been written down?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The gap between the pump and the basket<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
Gasoline is up 27.8% from a year ago, yet the inflation measures the Bank of Canada trusts most for underlying prices eased to 1.8% and 1.9% in September. Consumers are now forecasting 3.91% inflation over five years in the latest Bank of Canada survey. The gap between what is measured and what is expected is where investment decisions often go wrong.<br><br>
With a Bank of Canada rate decision on October 28, I am offering a short call to compare what these numbers mean for your portfolio and your borrowing. Would a 20 minute conversation this week be useful?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '[{"value":"27.8%","label":"Gasoline, year over year"},{"value":"1.9%","label":"CPI-median core, September"},{"value":"3.91%","label":"Consumer five-year expectations"},{"value":"4.0 pts","label":"City spread, Toronto to Whitehorse"}]',
