<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Rates apply above the top-bracket income threshold, $258,482 federally, with higher thresholds in British Columbia, Alberta, the Yukon and Newfoundland and Labrador. Capital losses offset capital gains only and are available only in non-registered accounts.</p>
<p>Those rates apply only above the top-bracket threshold: federal taxable income over $258,482 in most jurisdictions, with higher thresholds of $266,000 in British Columbia, $370,000 in Alberta, $500,000 in the Yukon and $1.14 million in Newfoundland and Labrador. An investor in a lower bracket recovers less per dollar of loss. In Ontario, the largest market, the top rate on a capital gain is 26.77%, which puts $10,000 of losses at $2,677.</p>
<h2>The Gain Side of the Ledger</h2>
<p>A loss is worth only what it offsets. Investors who rebalanced out of bank and energy winners this year, sold a business or a property, or realized gains from a concentrated position have gains to absorb it. The Alternative Minimum Tax is a separate calculation that can apply in years with large realized gains.</p>
<p>The planning sequence runs in a fixed order: confirm the realized gains for the year, find positions below adjusted cost base in non-registered accounts, check every account an affiliated person controls for the same security, and place the trades with enough room before December 30. A loss that fails any one of those steps has no tax value.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who have watched banks and energy climb are pleased, but a tax bill on realized gains, or a position still below cost, brings a different feeling: reluctance. Selling at a loss feels like admitting a mistake, even when the loss has real tax value. Others feel urgency without knowing the rules, and worry that one wrong purchase will cancel the benefit.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The last day to sell and have a loss count for 2026 is December 30, so we have 69 days. A loss is only useful against gains, so my first question is whether you have realized gains this year, or in the last three years, that it can offset. We would decide what to sell on investment merit first and treat the tax benefit as the bonus, not the reason. The rule to watch is the 61-day window: if you, your spouse or a corporation you control buys the same security within 30 days before or after the sale, the loss is denied. That includes automatic dividend reinvestment, and a repurchase inside your TFSA or RRSP loses it permanently. Let me run the list of positions below cost in your non-registered accounts, and then we will pick dates that leave room to finish well before the deadline.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Investors with realized gains in 2026 from rebalancing, concentrated positions, or the sale of a business or property, and anyone in the top bracket, where each dollar of loss recovers the most.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Investors with losses but no gains this year, who can still carry a loss back three years with Form T1A or forward indefinitely, and owners of corporate investment accounts, whose losses stay inside the corporation.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Investors with large gains in the prior three years who can carry a 2026 loss back, and those who sell by the end of October and can hold the same security again before year end.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Run an unrealized gain and loss report on every non-registered account and sort by adjusted cost base against market value.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Total each client 2026 realized gains to date, including rebalancing trades and any sale of property or a business.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check net capital gains reported for 2023, 2024 and 2025 to see whether a Form T1A carryback is available.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">List every dividend reinvestment plan and pre-authorized purchase on candidate securities and suspend them now, since purchases in the 30 days before a sale count.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Check spouse, partner and controlled corporation accounts for the same security before placing any sale.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm no RRSP, TFSA or FHSA trade will buy the same security within 30 days of the sale.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Record the adjusted cost base calculation for each lot sold.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Set a working deadline in each file for trades to be placed well before December 30, and book the follow-up calls.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Tax-loss selling and the December 30 deadline<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Thank you for your time today. Here is a short summary of what we discussed about tax-loss selling for 2026.<br><br>
The last trade date that settles in 2026 is December 30. A capital loss can offset capital gains only, and only in a non-registered account. Half of an allowable loss is used, and any amount we cannot use this year can be carried back three years or forward indefinitely.<br><br>
The main rule to protect is the 61-day window. If you, your spouse or a corporation you control buys the same security within 30 days before or after a sale, the loss is denied. Please do not buy any security we are considering selling, in any account, until we confirm the dates, and let me know about any automatic reinvestment you have set up.<br><br>
I will send the list of positions below cost and the proposed trade dates by the end of next week.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors with non-registered accounts holding positions below cost:</strong> most know a loss has value, few know the 61-day rule, and a repurchase inside a TFSA or RRSP voids the loss permanently.</p>
<p><strong>Investors who realized large gains in 2026:</strong> a business or property sale, or a concentrated position sold, where losses can offset the gain and the Alternative Minimum Tax may matter.</p>
<p><strong>Owners of corporate investment accounts:</strong> losses stay inside the corporation, and a personal loss cannot offset a corporate gain.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">With the TSX up about 21% over the past year, most portfolios have gains, and the positions still below cost are easy to miss. I am calling people with non-registered accounts about the December 30 trade deadline for 2026 losses, and a rule that can void them.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>Tax-loss selling is calendar-driven and rule-heavy: a fixed last trade date, a 61-day window, affiliated person rules and account-type traps. A self-directed investor can lose the whole benefit with one click, for example a repurchase inside a TFSA, and has no one to check it before the trade.</p>
<p>An advisor brings a process that starts with gains, not losses: what was realized this year, what can be carried back three years, and which accounts and family members hold the same security. With 69 days left, the prospect still has time to do it deliberately.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Do you hold any positions in a non-registered account that are worth less than you paid?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Have you sold anything this year at a gain, such as shares, a property or part of a business?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Do you know the last day to sell for a loss to count in 2026?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Does anyone in your household, or a company you control, hold the same investments?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Do you have dividend reinvestment turned on for any of those positions?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">If a loss were denied because of a repurchase, how would you find out, and who would catch it?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> A deadline that can save tax: December 30<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The TSX is up about 21% over the past year, so most portfolios have gains. That makes the positions still below cost easy to overlook, and a capital loss realized by December 30 can offset gains in a non-registered account.<br><br>
There is a catch: if you, your spouse or a corporation you control buys the same security within 30 days before or after the sale, the loss is denied. A repurchase inside a TFSA or RRSP loses it permanently.<br><br>
I would be glad to walk through your non-registered holdings in a 20 minute call before the deadline. Would one day this week or next work?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
