VALUES (
  '2026/10/22/markets-price-boc-hike-october-28-while-core-measures-sit-below-target',
  'economy',
  'article',
  'Markets Price a Bank of Canada Hike on October 28 While the Core Measures Sit Below Target',
  'Overnight swaps priced an 87% chance of an increase on October 19 as the Federal Reserve moved to 3.75% to 4.00%, yet CPI-trim and CPI-median are both below 2% and Canada lost 68,000 jobs in September.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>Overnight swaps priced an 87% chance of a quarter-point Bank of Canada hike on October 28,</strong><span> up from about 30% a week earlier, against a Bloomberg survey consensus that points to a hold.</span></li>
<li><strong>CPI-trim eased to 1.8% and CPI-median to 1.9% in September,</strong><span> while headline CPI rose to 3.1% because gasoline jumped 27.8%.</span></li>
<li><strong>Canada shed 68,000 jobs in September,</strong><span> the second straight monthly decline, and unemployment rose to 6.5% as the participation rate fell to 64.8%.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Federal Reserve raised its target range to 3.75% to 4.00% on September 16,</strong><span> widening the gap to the 2.25% Bank of Canada rate to 1.75 points.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Government of Canada five-year yield neared 4% on October 5,</strong><span> about 100 basis points above a year earlier, and it sets the pricing for five-year fixed mortgages.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The Bank of Canada announces its rate decision on October 28 with the overnight rate at 2.25%, where it has stood since October 29, 2025 across seven consecutive decisions. On October 19, overnight swaps priced an 87% chance of a quarter-point increase, up from about 30% a week earlier.</p>
<p>The core measures the Bank prefers point the other way. CPI-trim eased to 1.8% in September and CPI-median to 1.9%, both below the 2% target, while headline inflation rose to 3.1% on a 27.8% jump in gasoline prices.</p>
<p>Four signals disagree. Headline inflation says tighten, core inflation says wait, the labour market says ease, and a Federal Reserve that raised rates on September 16 has widened the gap to the Canadian policy rate. The October 28 decision, which comes with a full Monetary Policy Report, is where the Bank must reconcile all four in a single forecast.</p>
<h2>What the Core Measures Are Actually Saying</h2>
<p>September CPI came in at 3.1%, below the 3.2% consensus and 0.1 point above August. Excluding gasoline, prices rose 2.2%, down from 2.4%. Energy overall rose 19.0% and gasoline 27.8%, which accounts for the distance between the headline and everything else.</p>
<p>The measures designed to look through that distance moved down. CPI-trim eased to 1.8% from 1.9%, CPI-median to 1.9% from 2.0%, and CPI-common to 2.4% from 2.6%. Shelter costs rose 1.8% and food bought in stores rose 1.7%, the smallest annual gain since May 2024.</p>
<p>The reading is that the energy shock is in the headline and has not yet spread through the basket. That is a statement about September, and the Bank forecasts the next several quarters, not the last month.</p>
<h2>Why the Bank Still Expects a Higher Forecast</h2>
<p>On October 17, Governor Tiff Macklem told reporters he expects the Bank of Canada inflation forecast to go up, with oil far above the level assumed in July, and said there is no resolution in sight for the conflict in the Middle East.</p>
<p>Expectations are the channel that turns an energy shock into a persistent one. In the Bank of Canada consumer survey, five-year inflation expectations rose to 3.91% from 3.39% in the second quarter, matching short-term expectations. In its Business Outlook Survey, roughly half of firms plan to keep passing some or all cost increases on to customers. Most interviews for both surveys were completed before trade talks with the United States collapsed, and the Bank said expectations have risen alongside the tariffs and the rebound in energy.</p>
<h2>The Labour Market Argues the Other Way</h2>
<p>Canada shed 68,000 jobs in September after losing 42,000 in August, erasing more than half of the 181,000 jobs added between April and July. Unemployment rose to 6.5%. The participation rate fell to 64.8%, its lowest since December 1997 outside 2020.</p>
<p>TD senior economist Andrew Hencic wrote that two months of weak labour data between decisions would pour cold water on calls for near-term hikes. RBC economist Abbey Xu still expects the Bank to hold through 2026 and begin raising in 2027, while Scotiabank firmed up its call for a hike after the CPI release and Manulife forecasts increases in October and December. A recent Bloomberg survey of economists pointed to a hold, which leaves market pricing well ahead of the consensus forecast.</p>
<h2>The Fed Has Already Moved</h2>
<p>The Federal Reserve raised its target range by 25 basis points to 3.75% to 4.00% on September 16, a unanimous 12 to 0 vote and its first increase in three years. US CPI has since reached 3.7% for September, with gasoline up 34.2% and core inflation at 2.6%.</p>
<p>The Fed funds upper bound has returned to 4.00% while the Bank of Canada target has held at 2.25% since October 2025, leaving a policy rate gap of 1.75 points that matches the widest in the period shown.</p>
<div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
