VALUES (
  '2026/10/22/hormuz-flows-recover-to-82-percent-of-pre-war-levels-as-iran-reports-seven-tankers-hit',
  'geo',
  'article',
  'Hormuz Flows Recover to 82% of Pre-War Levels Through Convoys as Iran Reports Seven Tankers Hit',
  'Kpler data show Gulf crude moving at near pre-war volumes under US naval escort, while Iran claims strikes on seven tankers overnight, PortWatch counts about four tanker transits a day and Brent holds near $110.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>Iranian state television said seven tankers caught fire or were damaged overnight in the Strait of Hormuz,</strong><span> citing the Revolutionary Guard navy; the UK Maritime Trade Operations monitor did not report the attacks.</span></li>
<li><strong>Kpler estimates Hormuz crude flows at 11.7 million barrels a day in October,</strong><span> up from 9.75 million in September and about 82% of pre-war levels, mostly through US-escorted convoys near the Omani coast.</span></li>
<li><strong>IMF PortWatch data cited by industry reports put daily tanker transits at about 4 in the week of October 18,</strong><span> against a baseline near 85, a reading that conflicts with the Kpler volumes.</span></li>
<li><strong>Iran offered to reopen the strait within a week if the United States ends its blockade of Iranian ports,</strong><span> and President Trump rejected the seven-day proposal while indirect talks continue.</span></li>
<li><strong>Israel votes on October 27 and US midterms follow on November 3,</strong><span> with Brent near $110 a barrel, its highest since May and below the March peak of $118.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>Two pictures of the Strait of Hormuz were in circulation on October 22, and they do not agree. Iranian state television said seven oil tankers caught fire or were damaged overnight from strikes or naval mines. Kpler data cited by Bloomberg show crude moving through the strait at about 82% of pre-war volumes.</p>
<p>Both can be partly true. Cargoes are getting through under naval protection, while the cost, risk and verification of each passage have risen sharply. For anyone pricing energy risk, the disagreement is the story.</p>
<h2>What Iran Claims and What Has Been Confirmed</h2>
<p>The attack claims came from Iranian state television, citing the Revolutionary Guard navy, which gave no details on casualties. The UK Maritime Trade Operations monitor did not report the incidents and noted that some shippers are reluctant to disclose them. Independent verification of earlier claims has been thin as well: industry reports on three supertanker mine strikes in the past week note that evidence remains elusive.</p>
<p>The monitor did record a record 12 attacks in or around the strait last week, according to Reuters columnist Ron Bousso, and believes the real number is higher. The Guard navy also warned it will strike US bases in the region if the United States keeps guiding commercial ships through the strait.</p>
<h2>The Data Disagree</h2>
<p>Kpler estimates Hormuz crude flows at 11.7 million barrels a day in October, up from 9.75 million in September and under 1 million at the April low. Middle East exports overall reached 17.3 million barrels a day, above the pre-war average of 16.9 million. Most of the recovery moves in convoys along the Omani coast under US naval protection, and about a third of crossings use the northern route controlled by Iran, where flows are opaque.</p>
<p>IMF PortWatch, as cited in industry reports, shows a different picture: daily tanker transits fell from a baseline near 85 to about 4 in the week of October 18. Those two readings measure different things, one barrels and one vessel counts, and they have not been reconciled publicly. A reading of both is that volumes held up on fewer, larger and more heavily escorted passages, but the data do not prove that.</p>
<div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
<div style="background:#f5f5f5;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d0d0;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
<span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#111;letter-spacing:0.02em;">MIDDLE EAST CRUDE EXPORTS VS HORMUZ FLOWS</span>
<span style="font-size:20px;font-weight:700;color:#111;">82% OF PRE-WAR</span>
<span style="font-size:13px;color:#2e7d32;">▲ 1.95M BPD SINCE SEPT</span>
<span style="font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-left:auto;">KPLER, MILLIONS OF BARRELS PER DAY &nbsp;|&nbsp; SEP TO OCT 2026</span>
</div>
<div style="padding:12px 14px 8px;">
<script>
(function(){
  var _cs = document.currentScript;
  var NS = "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg";
  var FONT = "-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif";
  function el(tag, attrs, txt){
    var e = document.createElementNS(NS, tag);
    for (var k in attrs) e.setAttribute(k, attrs[k]);
    if (txt !== undefined) e.textContent = txt;
    return e;
  }
  function tx(x, y, s, anchor, size, fill, weight){
    return el("text", {x: x, y: y, "text-anchor": anchor, "font-size": size, "font-weight": weight || 400, fill: fill, "font-family": FONT}, s);
  }
  var svg = document.createElementNS(NS, "svg");
  svg.setAttribute("viewBox","0 0 680 300");
  svg.setAttribute("width","100%");
  var data = [{c:"Pre-war exports",v:16.9,f:"#4a5568"},{c:"Sept exports",v:16.4,f:"#4a5568"},{c:"Oct exports",v:17.3,f:"#4a5568"},{c:"Hormuz, Sept",v:9.75,f:"#6b7280"},{c:"Hormuz, Oct",v:11.7,f:"#6b7280"}];
  var margin = {left: 110, right: 24, top: 18, bottom: 46};
  var W = 680, H = 300;
  var PW = W - margin.left - margin.right;
  var PH = H - margin.top - margin.bottom;
  var n = data.length;
  var gap = 12;
  var barH = Math.floor((PH - (n - 1) * gap) / n);
  var hi = 20;
  function xp(v){ return margin.left + v / hi * PW; }
  function rowTop(i){ return margin.top + i * (barH + gap); }
  function rowMid(i){ return rowTop(i) + barH / 2; }
  var plotL = margin.left, plotR = margin.left + PW, plotT = margin.top, plotB = margin.top + PH;

  // 1. gridlines
  for (var t = 0; t < hi; t += 5){
    svg.appendChild(el("line", {x1: xp(t), x2: xp(t), y1: plotT, y2: plotB, stroke: "#ececec", "stroke-width": 0.5}));
  }
  // 2. reference line: pre-war average exports
  var refX = xp(data[0].v);
  svg.appendChild(el("line", {x1: refX, x2: refX, y1: plotT, y2: plotB, stroke: "#1a3560", "stroke-opacity": 0.5, "stroke-width": 1, "stroke-dasharray": "2,3"}));
  // 3. bars
  for (var i = 0; i < n; i++){
    svg.appendChild(el("rect", {x: plotL, y: rowTop(i), width: xp(data[i].v) - plotL, height: barH, fill: data[i].f}));
  }
  // 4. axis lines
  svg.appendChild(el("line", {x1: plotL, x2: plotR, y1: plotB, y2: plotB, stroke: "#d8d8d8", "stroke-width": 1}));
  svg.appendChild(el("line", {x1: plotL, x2: plotL, y1: plotT, y2: plotB, stroke: "#d8d8d8", "stroke-width": 1}));
  // 6. gold pill on the October Hormuz bar tip, left of the tip
  var last = n - 1;
  var pillText = data[last].v.toFixed(2);
  var pillW = Math.ceil(pillText.length * 9 * 0.58) + 10;
  var pillH = 16;
  var pillX = xp(data[last].v) - pillW - 6;
  var pillY = rowMid(last) - pillH / 2;
  if (pillX < plotL) pillX = plotL;
  svg.appendChild(el("rect", {x: pillX, y: pillY, width: pillW, height: pillH, rx: 3, fill: "#e8a825"}));
  svg.appendChild(tx(pillX + pillW / 2, pillY + pillH / 2 + 3, pillText, "middle", 9, "#111111", 700));
  // 7. labels and annotations
  for (var r = 0; r < n; r++){
    svg.appendChild(tx(margin.left - 5, rowMid(r) + 3, data[r].c, "end", 8.5, "#aaaaaa", 400));
    if (r !== last) svg.appendChild(tx(xp(data[r].v) - 6, rowMid(r) + 3, data[r].v.toFixed(2), "end", 8.5, "#ffffff", 700));
  }
  svg.appendChild(tx(plotL + 8, rowMid(last) + 3, "ABOUT 1 IN 3 CROSSINGS USE THE IRANIAN ROUTE", "start", 8, "#ffffff", 400));
  var refLabel = "PRE-WAR AVERAGE";
  var refW = Math.ceil(refLabel.length * 7 * 0.68);
  var nearRight = (refX + refW + 4) > plotR;
  svg.appendChild(tx(nearRight ? refX - 4 : refX + 4, plotB - 6, refLabel, nearRight ? "end" : "start", 7, "#1a3560", 700));
  for (var k = 0; k < hi; k += 5){
    svg.appendChild(tx(xp(k), plotB + 14, k + "", "middle", 8, "#999999", 400));
  }
  svg.appendChild(tx(margin.left + PW / 2, plotB + 30, "Millions of barrels per day (Kpler)", "middle", 8, "#999999", 400));
  _cs.parentNode.appendChild(svg);
})();
</script>
</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Kpler data cited by Bloomberg and columnist Ron Bousso, October 21 to 22, 2026. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Middle East crude exports and Hormuz flows in millions of barrels per day, from Kpler. The pre-war average covers the two months before the February 28 strikes, and October figures are month to date.</p>
<h2>Why Recovery Is Not Normal</h2>
<p>Benchmark rates for a tanker carrying two million barrels from the Middle East to China sit near an all-time high of about $1.4 million a day, more than 12 times pre-war levels. Tanker insurance has risen to roughly 10% of cargo value from about 3%. Saudi Aramco and ADNOC have begun selling some cargoes delivered to buyers in China and India instead of at the loading point, shifting transport risk onto the seller. Bousso calls the result a new abnormal: high volumes at a high cost.</p>
<p>Brent trades near $110 a barrel, its highest since May and below the $118 wartime peak reached in March. Hapag-Lloyd estimates about six weeks would be needed to restore normal shipping after a reopening, so even a diplomatic breakthrough would not clear the costs quickly.</p>
<h2>The Diplomatic Clock</h2>
<p>Iran proposed ending the war and fully reopening the strait within a week if the United States meets several conditions, including ending its blockade of Iranian ports. The terms echo a June deal that collapsed within days. President Trump rejected the seven-day proposal, though indirect discussions through Pakistani and Qatari mediators continue and attacks on ships have increased since then.</p>
<p>The United States is sending a third aircraft carrier and thousands of additional troops. Israel holds an election on October 27 and the US midterms follow on November 3, where prices linked to the war have angered voters. Bousso reports that Trump has said the United States will not attack Iran before the polls.</p>
<p>The window to the first week of November therefore looks like a pause with armed forces in place, not a settlement. Energy markets are pricing that gap.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients are unsettled by headlines about burning tankers and carriers moving into the region, and by the price of gasoline. Some are asking whether to sell equities or buy energy. Others have heard that oil is flowing again and cannot reconcile that with the news. Most want to know whether the war is about to get worse and what it means for their savings.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">There are two things happening at once. Oil is moving through the strait at close to pre-war volumes, but only with naval escorts and at a much higher cost, and Iran says it hit seven tankers overnight, which has not been independently confirmed. Brent is near 110 dollars, which is high but below the March peak. Nobody can predict whether the next two weeks bring a deal or an escalation, including me. What I can do is make sure your portfolio can tolerate both. Let us look at how much of your plan depends on energy prices staying where they are, how much of your equity exposure is energy and how much is companies that pay for fuel, and whether you hold enough cash and short-term bonds so that you never have to sell during a bad week.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with large energy holdings or resource-heavy Canadian equity portfolios, and clients on fixed incomes who feel fuel and food prices directly.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients holding airlines, transportation and consumer names that pay for fuel, and clients with US dollar assets, since a stronger US dollar in a risk-off week lifts their value in Canadian terms.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Savers with short-term cash and bonds, who can add at higher yields, and long-term investors with enough cash on hand to avoid selling in a drawdown.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull each client energy weighting across direct holdings, funds and the Canadian equity benchmark and note the combined exposure.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients with a withdrawal planned in the next 12 months and confirm the cash or short-term bond reserve that covers it.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Mark October 27 and November 3 on the calendar and book check-in calls with the most anxious clients before those dates.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a one-page note that sets out the two readings of Hormuz flows (Kpler volumes and PortWatch transits) and what each implies.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review fixed-income clients for exposure to inflation from energy and note any duration risk.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document each client conversation, including any request to sell and the reason you gave for or against it.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What the Strait of Hormuz headlines mean for your plan<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Thank you for speaking with me today. Here is a short summary of what we discussed about oil and the situation around the Strait of Hormuz.<br><br>
Oil is moving through the strait at close to pre-war volumes, but under naval escort and at a high cost, and Iran has claimed new attacks on tankers that have not been independently confirmed. Brent is near 110 dollars a barrel. Your plan was built to handle periods like this, and we will review your energy exposure and your cash reserve to confirm that.<br><br>
I will contact you after the Israeli election on October 27 and again after the US midterms on November 3, or sooner if anything changes materially.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Business owners with fuel or freight costs:</strong> diesel, jet fuel and shipping costs track Brent, and many have not planned for a sustained 110 dollar oil price.</p>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors with large energy weightings:</strong> Canadian index funds and individual holdings are heavily exposed to oil, and many cannot tell how much of their return depends on one outcome in the Gulf.</p>
<p><strong>Pre-retirees drawing down within three years:</strong> a sharp energy-driven selloff in the first years of withdrawals does the most lasting damage, and few hold a dedicated reserve.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Oil is back near 110 dollars, tankers are being reported hit in the Gulf, and two elections in the next two weeks could change the direction. I am calling people who manage their own investments to ask how much of their plan depends on one energy outcome.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>Headline risk is easy to see and hard to size. A self-directed investor can read about the Strait of Hormuz but has no neutral way to measure how much of a portfolio, a business or a retirement plan rides on it.</p>
<p>An advisor can size it: how much energy exposure, how much cash, how much time before the money is needed. That turns a news cycle into a plan with a defined response, and the dates on the calendar this month give a natural reason to start.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<div class="checklist-item">How much of your portfolio is in energy, directly or through index funds?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Do you have money you will need in the next three years, and where is it held?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">If oil rose another 20 dollars or fell back to 80, what would change for you?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Does your business depend on fuel or freight costs, and have you priced a sustained high oil price?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Have the recent headlines caused you to want to change anything, and if so, what?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Who do you talk to when the news and the data disagree?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Oil near 110 dollars and what it means for your plan<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
Oil is trading near 110 dollars a barrel, tanker traffic through the Strait of Hormuz is disputed, and Israel and the United States both hold elections in the next two weeks. Energy affects portfolios, businesses and retirement income in different ways.<br><br>
I am offering a short call to look at how much of your plan depends on energy prices and what a reasonable response would be under each outcome. Would 20 minutes this week work?<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
