VALUES (
  '2026/10/22/tsx-recovers-monday-rate-repricing-as-all-six-big-banks-rally-still-2-percent-below-august-record',
  'market',
  'article',
  'TSX Recovers Monday Rate Repricing as All Six Big Banks Rally, Still 2% Below August Record',
  'The S&P/TSX Composite rose 0.60% to 36,324.99 on October 21 for a second straight gain, led by financials and industrials, while energy was flat with Brent near $110 and the Bank of Canada decision a week away.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The S&P/TSX Composite closed at 36,324.99 on October 21, up 217.03 points or 0.60%,</strong><span> its highest close since September 22 and above the October 16 close of 36,176.55.</span></li>
<li><strong>All six big banks advanced, led by TD at 1.98% and National Bank at 1.90%,</strong><span> and financials gained 1.28% as industrials rose 1.12% and consumer discretionary fell 1.08%.</span></li>
<li><strong>Energy was nearly flat at 0.06% even with Brent near $110,</strong><span> and breadth was positive at 1,505 advancers to 702 decliners on 411.15 million shares.</span></li>
<li><strong>The index sits about 2.0% below its August record of 37,069.11</strong><span> and 3.7% above the October 7 low of 35,041.86.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Bank of Canada decides on October 28 and markets lean toward a hike,</strong><span> with the federal budget due on November 5 and Israel voting on October 27.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The S&amp;P/TSX Composite rose 217.03 points, or 0.60%, to 36,324.99 on October 21, its second straight gain. On Monday October 19 it fell 0.69% to 35,928.63 as Bank of Canada hike odds jumped, and the close is now above the 36,176.55 level it left on Friday October 16.</p>
<p>The recovery is narrower than the headline suggests. Banks and industrials did the work, energy contributed almost nothing, and the index is still about 2.0% below its August record of 37,069.11.</p>
<h2>Banks Led the Session</h2>
<p>All six big banks advanced. TD led at 1.98%, followed by National Bank at 1.90%, BMO at 1.66%, Scotiabank at 1.63%, CIBC at 1.40% and RBC at 1.23%. Financials gained 1.28% as a sector and industrials gained 1.12%. Manulife rose 1.57% to $63.51.</p>
<p>Trading Economics attributed the move to eased pressure on credit-sensitive stocks and a pause in the oil rally. A separate report tied the industrial and financial strength to a $9 billion support package for the steel and auto industries announced by Canada and Ontario. The package is reported by one source and its size has not been confirmed elsewhere.</p>
<h2>Energy Sat Out</h2>
<p>Energy was nearly flat at 0.06% even as crude stayed volatile. Trading Economics quotes showed WTI near $99 and Brent near $111, up 1.7% and 2.5% respectively on the day, while other reports put Brent at about $110. Consumer discretionary fell 1.08% and telecommunications fell 0.95%. Magna International fell 4.1% after two analysts cut price targets.</p>
<p>Breadth was positive, with 1,505 advancers against 702 decliners on volume of 411.15 million shares, about 1.8% above Tuesday. A rally led by banks with energy flat has a different character from one led by oil, because it does not depend on the war premium.</p>
<div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
