</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Daily closing levels of the S&P/TSX Composite from September 16 to October 21, 2026. The dashed horizontal line marks the August record close of 37,069.11, and the vertical line marks October 19, when the index fell 0.69%.</p>
<h2>Where the Index Sits in the Range</h2>
<p>The index is 3.7% above the October 7 closing low of 35,041.86, set after a 1.7% decline that day, and below the September 22 close of 36,335.61. In the five weeks shown, it has ranged between those two levels, with the largest daily falls 1.7% on October 7 and 1.6% on September 23, and the largest gain 1.55% on October 15.</p>
<p>Wall Street also rose. The Dow gained 0.69% to 52,856.09, the S&amp;P 500 gained 0.58% to 7,757.17 and the Nasdaq Composite gained 1.05% to 26,702.76. One report noted that new 52-week lows still outnumbered new highs on both the NYSE and Nasdaq, which suggests the US recovery is also incomplete.</p>
<h2>The Week Ahead</h2>
<p>The Bank of Canada decides on October 28 with a Monetary Policy Report, and markets lean toward a quarter-point increase even though a Bloomberg survey of economists points to a hold. Israel holds its election on October 27, US midterms follow on November 3, and the federal budget is due on November 5. The CFIB business barometer is released on October 22 at 8:00 a.m. ET.</p>
<p>The index has now recovered the Monday drop that came with the jump in hike odds. If the Bank raises rates on October 28 with core inflation below target, banks and rate-sensitive sectors will reprice again, and the dependence on one decision is the risk in this rally.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients see the index back above 36,000 and wonder whether the worry is over, while the news still carries war and rate headlines. Some feel they missed the rebound after the Monday drop. Others, holding bank stocks, are pleased with the gain but anxious about the Bank of Canada decision next week. Most want to know whether to do anything.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The Canadian market rose 0.6 percent yesterday and has recovered the Monday decline, led by the big banks, which all rose. It is still about 2 percent below its August high. That tells me the market is calm for now, not that the risks have gone. The Bank of Canada decides on October 28, there are elections in Israel and the United States in the next two weeks, and oil is near 110 dollars. I would not change your plan because of one good day, and I would not have changed it after the bad one on Monday. What I want to confirm is that your portfolio is not too reliant on any one of these outcomes, particularly bank stocks and energy. Let us look at your allocation and make sure it still matches your goals and time horizon.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients whose Canadian equity holdings are concentrated in the six big banks or in financials, and anyone who planned to buy after the Monday decline and has not yet acted.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with balanced portfolios, who gained modestly, and clients with energy holdings, who saw almost no change on the day.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Investors with cash waiting to be deployed, who can use a staged approach around the October 28 decision, and clients with a long horizon, for whom one session changes nothing.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Calculate each client weighting in the six big banks and in financials as a share of the Canadian equity sleeve.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag any portfolio where banks plus energy exceed the concentration limit in the investment policy statement.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">List clients with cash earmarked for deployment and agree a staged schedule that does not depend on October 28.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a one-page note showing the index range from September 16 to October 21 and the August record.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Calendar October 27, October 28, November 3 and November 5 for client communication.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document each conversation, including any decision not to trade and the reason.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The market this week and what it means for your plan<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Thank you for speaking with me today. Here is a short summary of what we covered about the market this week.<br><br>
The Canadian market rose on Wednesday and has recovered the Monday decline, led by the big banks. It is still about 2 percent below its August high. The Bank of Canada decides on October 28, and there are elections in Israel and the United States in the next two weeks. Your plan was built with periods like this in mind, and we agreed not to make changes in response to a single day.<br><br>
I will review your bank and energy weightings and contact you after the Bank of Canada decision.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Self-directed investors with Canadian bank stocks as core holdings:</strong> the sector just gained over 1% in a day and is exposed to the October 28 decision, and many have never measured the concentration.</p>
<p><strong>Investors who sold or paused on Monday and are sitting in cash:</strong> the index recovered the loss within two sessions, and they have no plan for when to return.</p>
<p><strong>Pre-retirees comparing a TSX near 36,000 against their retirement date:</strong> they want to know whether to wait for a new record, which sits 2% away.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">The TSX bounced back this week, led by the banks, and is about 2 percent from its record, with a Bank of Canada decision on October 28. I am calling people who manage their own investments to ask how they are positioned for that decision.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A market that moves 0.7% down on Monday and 0.6% up on Wednesday teaches self-directed investors to react to headlines. An advisor replaces that with a written plan for concentration, cash and timing that holds up through either outcome.</p>
<p>The week ahead has a decision, two elections and a budget. That calendar gives a natural reason to review exposure before the next move, not after it.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
